  "data": {
    "A": "Research dimension reduction and memory locality. Normalize the matrix so band boundaries span d=min(m,n) and each band scans L=max(m,n), reducing work to O(d²L). Compare two exact layouts: contiguous boundary-prefix arrays, allowing each band to scan two contiguous arrays; and contiguous matrix rows with incremental band sums for each starting boundary. For incremental sums, compare fusing accumulation with prefix counting against a separate vectorizable accumulation pass. Initially retain standard-map counting to isolate layout effects. Secondary improvements may include unsynchronized C++ input and removing demonstrably redundant initialization. Preserve negative-value handling and count every nonempty rectangle exactly once. Deliver the best correct standalone program with measured evidence and describe its band-counting interface for integration.",
    "B": "Research an allocation-free prefix-frequency kernel. Replace unordered_map with a reusable, power-of-two open-addressed table using exact key comparisons and a suitable integer hash. Allocate capacity at least twice L+1, because a band inserts at most L+1 distinct prefixes; unsuccessful target lookups must not insert entries. Compare generation stamps, touched-slot clearing and full clearing under the unchanged measurements. Seed frequency[0]=1; for every prefix s, first add frequency[s-k], then increment frequency[s], including when k=0. Handle collisions and reset state exactly. Signed 32-bit keys are safe under the official bounds: |s-k| is at most 1,098,001,000; frequencies fit 32 bits, but answers require 64 bits. Use unsigned arithmetic for hash mixing. First isolate the kernel improvement from layout and threading changes. Deliver a standalone candidate and a reusable private-workspace design.",
    "C": "Research balanced parallel execution on the four ARM64 CPU cores. Bands are independent: assign each pair of boundaries exactly once, give every worker private prefix-frequency storage and a 64-bit subtotal, and reduce only after completion. Avoid atomics, allocation and thread creation inside band loops. Compare weighted partitions of starting boundaries, accounting for their decreasing numbers of ending boundaries, with chunks of flattened triangular pair indices backed by boundary-prefix arrays. Incremental-sum implementations must retain a starting boundary's state or explicitly rebuild it when splitting work. Compare serial, two-worker and four-worker execution within the existing budget; investigate a work-based serial cutoff to avoid overhead on small inputs. Use only threading facilities supported by the unchanged compiler/build configuration. Deliver measured scheduling choices, workspace requirements and a correct serial fallback.",
    "G": "Integrate only programs actually produced by A/B/C. Preserve standalone winners and compare compatible combinations within the fixed evaluation budget: normalized contiguous layouts, private flat tables and balanced parallel scheduling. Resolve layout and scheduler dependencies explicitly; do not split incremental-band state without reconstruction. Validate each measured integration with the unchanged oracle and correctness suite. Audit zero initialization, lookup-before-insertion for k=0, negative keys, collision handling, rectangular orientation, single-row/single-column inputs, complete task coverage and 64-bit reductions. Compare end-to-end performance against both standalone inputs and the baseline: gains are not additive, and integration may regress. Select the fastest measured correct candidate, including a standalone or baseline fallback. Report provenance and actual measurements; change no tests, oracle, compiler configuration, budgets, target or permissions.",
    "analysis": "The relevant task is Problem A, submatrix-sum counting; A/B/C are research roles, not the other contest problems. The supplied baseline is correct on four checked cases and averages 0.250757 seconds across three closely grouped measurements. No workload dimensions or profiling evidence are supplied. Source-grounded hypotheses are unnecessary O(m*n²) work when n>m, strided prefix-array access, repeated hash-table allocation/lookups, and unexploited band independence. The target provides four ARM64 CPU cores and no GPU. Separate layout/algorithm, counting-kernel and scheduling research before measuring integration. Every valid input must remain correct, including negative entries and k=0; the maximum possible answer is 249,500,250,000, requiring 64-bit accumulation. Performance choices must follow general input properties and this run's measurements, never test identities or expected answers."
